---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "momentous protein powder",
      "momentous essential whey",
      "momentous protein review",
      "momentous plant protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Isolate Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-isolate/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Momentous Protein Powder: Certified, but Small</Fragment>
      Momentous sells some of the most carefully documented protein on the market, in a scoop that is smaller and dearer than most. Here is what the label delivers, which tub to buy, and when a cheaper certified isolate does the same job.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-it">Who Makes Momentous</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#whey-label">The Whey Label, Line by Line</a>
      <a href="#nsf">What NSF Certification Buys</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#plant-recovery">Plant Protein and Recovery</a>
      <a href="#vs">Momentous vs the Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#where-to-buy">Amazon vs Buying Direct</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Momentous Whey Protein Isolate is a clean, NSF Certified for Sport powder with a short ingredient list and an unusually small scoop: 20 g of protein in about 90 calories. If you compete in a drug-tested sport, that certification is worth real money. If you do not, you are paying a premium of roughly a dollar per 25 g of protein over equally clean isolates, and this review shows exactly where that money goes.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy <strong>Momentous Whey Protein Isolate</strong> if certification is the thing you are paying for. Each scoop delivers 20 g of protein from grass-fed whey isolate in about 90 calories, per the brand's label at the time of writing, and the product carries NSF Certified for Sport status. Momentous also publishes certificates of analysis by batch, which is more paperwork than most brands offer and the main reason it appears on our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> shortlist.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the dose and the price. Twenty grams is a light serving for a whey isolate. Most competitors put 25 to 28 g in a scoop, so you either accept less protein per shake or use more powder and burn through the tub faster. At list price, a 25-serving jar works out to roughly $3.50 per 25 g of protein, falling to about $2.60 on the brand's subscription discount. Mainstream wheys sit well under half the full-price figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the range is narrower. The 100% Plant Protein is a sensible certified option for vegans at 20 g per scoop. Recovery adds 10 g of carbohydrate to the same whey and costs more per gram of protein than anything else Momentous sells. There is no Momentous casein powder at the time of writing, so slow-digesting bedtime protein has to come from somewhere else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who should skip Momentous entirely? Anyone shopping on price, anyone who wants 25 g or more per scoop without a second scoop, and anyone not subject to drug testing who would be equally happy with a cheaper isolate carrying a different independent certification. For those buyers, the alternatives at the bottom of the product list do the same job for less.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Momentous Whey Isolate at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop (label)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">90</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">NSF</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Certified for Sport</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~$3.50</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein, list price</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Momentous
          </h2>

          <p>
            Momentous is a performance-nutrition company founded in 2018 by Jeff Byers and Erica Good, who serve as chief executive and president. In early 2024 the consumer investment firm Humble Growth took a significant minority stake, a deal reported at about $32 million. The company is privately held and sells mainly through its own website, with distribution through specialty retailers, gyms and sporting-goods stores.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand's whole pitch is certification. Its product pages lead with the NSF Certified for Sport mark rather than flavor, and its marketing is aimed squarely at athletes, military users and buyers who listen to performance podcasts. That focus is the reason to consider it, and it also explains the pricing: NSF certification means product lots are screened for a long list of banned substances and checked against the label, and that testing is paid for in the price of the jar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whoever blends and packs a supplement, the buyer usually has no way to inspect the factory. That is the practical case for independent certification: the NSF program audits the facility and tests finished product, so you are not relying solely on a brand's own quality team or its marketing. Momentous has built its identity around submitting to that process, and it is the main thing you are paying for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The product names have shifted over time. The whey has been sold as Essential Grass-Fed Whey and is now listed on the brand's site as Whey Protein Isolate, with the older Essential name still showing up on some retailer and Amazon listings. The plant powder has likewise appeared as Essential Plant-Based Protein and now as 100% Plant Protein. If you see both names, check the protein figure and the NSF mark rather than assuming they are different products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Momentous Protein Range, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Momentous sells creatine, sleep formulas and collagen alongside its proteins, but three products are genuine protein powders, and those are ranked below for the job of hitting a daily protein target, followed by two certified alternatives from other brands. Figures come from Momentous product pages and retailer labels at the time of writing and vary slightly by flavor, so check the tub you buy. The ranking is built on label analysis, certification status and approximate cost per 25 g of protein, not on our own lab or taste tests.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whey-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Whey Isolate Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the protein. Momentous lists 20 g of protein per serving from roughly 23 to 26 g of powder depending on flavor, which means the scoop is around 76 to 85 percent protein by weight. That is normal for a flavored isolate. The low number is about scoop size, not dilution: Momentous simply chose a smaller serving than most brands do.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein source is grass-fed whey protein isolate from European dairy farms, which the brand says come from cows raised without rBST or other hormones. It describes the whey as cold-processed and micro-filtered. Those are reasonable claims for a premium isolate, but none of them changes the amino acid content much. Whey isolate carries roughly 10 to 11 percent leucine, so a 20 g serving supplies around 2 g, close to the threshold usually cited for maximizing a muscle-building response in younger adults.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each serving also contains 200 mg of ProHydrolase, an enzyme blend added to help break the protein down. The evidence that added enzymes meaningfully improve muscle growth from an isolate is thin, because isolate is already very easy to digest. Treat it as harmless rather than a reason to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweeteners vary by flavor. Chocolate and vanilla use monk fruit extract and stevia, strawberry uses Reb M, a purified stevia compound, and unflavored has no sweetener at all. There is no sucralose or acesulfame potassium, no added sugar, and no gums or thickeners listed in the core flavors. Calories sit at about 90 per scoop, with very little carbohydrate or fat.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Momentous Whey Isolate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical mainstream whey</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Monk fruit and stevia (most flavors)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Certification</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies; often none</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Servings per jar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 30 or more</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Flavor range is modest: chocolate, vanilla, strawberry and unflavored make up the core, with limited editions such as mint chocolate, spiced chai and chocolate coconut that come and go and are sometimes restricted to certain sizes. Buyer feedback generally describes the chocolate and vanilla as mild rather than dessert-sweet, which suits people who dislike heavy sweetener aftertaste and disappoints people who want candy-bar flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nsf" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What NSF Certified for Sport Actually Buys You
          </h2>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport is the strictest common certification for supplements in North America. It combines a label-claim check, contaminant screening and testing for a long list of substances banned by sports bodies, with facility audits and ongoing lot testing. It is the mark recognized by several major North American professional leagues and many collegiate programs, which is why drug-tested athletes are often told to buy only from the NSF database.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an athlete who can be suspended for a positive test, that matters more than anything on the nutrition panel. Contamination with stimulants or anabolic agents is rare in protein powder, but it happens, and an athlete is responsible for whatever is in their body. Paying an extra dollar per shake is cheap insurance against a season-ending mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the value is smaller but real. Certification confirms that the protein you pay for is in the tub, which protects against amino spiking, and it adds contaminant screening. However, plenty of cheaper whey isolates carry Informed Choice, Informed Sport or published third-party heavy-metal results that cover most of what a non-athlete cares about. NSF is the gold standard; it is not the only acceptable standard.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check the database, not the jar.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Certifications apply to specific products and sometimes specific flavors. Before buying for a drug-tested sport, look the exact product and flavor up on the NSF Certified for Sport website or app, because a mark on an old photo or a reseller listing is not proof that the tub in front of you is covered.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One more point in Momentous's favor: the brand publishes batch certificates of analysis and lists third-party test results through Light Labs on the product page. That is above the industry norm. If your interest in certification is mostly about heavy metals rather than banned substances, read those results directly and compare them with the published numbers from brands such as Transparent Labs, which also posts lab reports. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains how to read them.
          </p>
        </div>

