        <div id="plant-recovery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Plant Protein and Recovery: Worth Buying?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">100% Plant Protein</h3>

          <p>
            The plant powder delivers 20 g of protein from a blend of PURIS pea protein and organic brown rice protein, sourced from the United States and Canada according to Momentous, in about 110 to 120 calories. Pea and rice are the standard complementary pairing because rice covers some of pea's methionine shortfall. It is NSF Certified for Sport, vegan and gluten-free per the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-offs are the usual plant ones. Plant blends carry less leucine per gram than whey, so 20 g of pea and rice is a slightly weaker muscle-building dose than 20 g of whey isolate, and you may want a scoop and a half to compensate. Each flavor also contains a small amount of organic cane sugar, about 1 to 2 g per serving, alongside stevia, monk fruit or Reb M. At roughly $2.85 per 25 g of protein at list price, it is mid-premium for a plant powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are vegan and need certification, it is one of the few plant powders that ticks the NSF box, and it is easy to recommend. If you only need dairy-free protein and do not compete, compare it with the options in our <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">OWYN review</a> and our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide, where uncertified plant blends deliver more protein for less.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recovery</h3>

          <p>
            Recovery is the same grass-fed whey isolate paired with 10 g of carbohydrate from dextrose and rice maltodextrin, giving 20 g of protein and 140 to 160 calories per two-scoop serving, with about 5 g of added sugar. The idea is a 2 to 1 protein-to-carbohydrate post-workout drink. It is NSF Certified for Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most lifters the carbohydrate adds little. Unless you train twice a day or do long endurance sessions, your next meal replaces glycogen just as well, and a banana with a whey shake costs far less than Recovery's roughly $4.60 per 25 g of protein. It makes sense for an endurance athlete in heavy training who wants a single certified product to throw in a bag. Stock has also been patchy, with the brand's page noting that a restock is due.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Momentous vs Thorne vs Transparent Labs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The closest rival is <strong>Thorne Whey Protein Isolate</strong>, which also carries NSF Certified for Sport status and a short ingredient list, at about 21 g of protein per scoop. Thorne has a long clinical and pro-sport reputation, and its isolate is the natural cross-shop for anyone considering Momentous. The two are close on dose and price; choose on flavor preference and current price. Our <a href="/thorne-protein-powder-review/">Thorne protein review</a> runs the same numbers for its range.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Transparent Labs Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate</strong> takes a different route. It does not carry NSF, but the brand states that it is certified by Informed Choice and publishes batch lab results, and it packs 28 g of protein into a scoop from American grass-fed dairy. That bigger dose drops the cost per 25 g of protein to roughly $2 at its list price for a 2 lb tub, well under Momentous at full price. Read the <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs review</a> for the full breakdown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Legion Whey+</strong>, another popular premium isolate, Momentous wins on certification and loses on dose. Legion lists 22 g of protein from Irish grass-fed whey and relies on Labdoor testing rather than a sport certification. That suits recreational buyers but not drug-tested athletes. See our <a href="/legion-protein-powder-review/">Legion protein review</a> for details.
          </p>

